  "answer_text": "Great question! Here is a comprehensive overview of what opioids are derived from:\n\n---\n\n## What Are Opioids Derived From?\n\nOpioids are a class of drugs derived from substances that occur naturally in the **opium poppy** (*Papaver somniferum*) and are widely used for pain relief and sedation. More specifically, in its raw state, opium is a gummy latex extracted from *Papaver somniferum*.\n\nCultivation of the opium poppy for its active constituents is thought to have begun about 3000 BCE, and opioids that occur naturally in the plant have been used specifically for relieving pain and for producing euphoria since at least the time of the ancient Greeks.\n\n---\n\n## Three Categories of Opioids\n\nThere are three main types of opioids based on how they are derived:\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udf3f Natural Opioids\nNatural opioids are substances derived directly from the opium poppy, typically from the resin of the plant's seed pod. Natural opioids are also referred to as **opiates**. Some opioid drugs are made from naturally occurring plant compounds (alkaloids) that come from a specific type of poppy plant called an opium poppy. Natural opioids, such as **codeine**, **heroin**, **morphine**, and **opium**, can be extracted from the opium poppy.\n\n### 2. \ud83e\uddea Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids are made by chemically processing natural opioids, modifying them into new opioid compounds. For pharmaceutical-grade opioids, semi-synthetic opioid painkillers may be made in modern laboratory and manufacturing facilities. While these drugs are man-made and don't occur in nature, natural opioids from the opium poppy serve as the \"raw material\" that pharmaceutical manufacturers (or drug traffickers) use to make semi-synthetic opioids. A few opioid molecules \u2014 such as **hydrocodone** (e.g., Vicodin), **hydromorphone** (e.g., Dilaudid), and **oxycodone** (e.g., OxyContin, Percocet) \u2014 may be partially synthesized from chemical components of opium.\n\n### 3. \u2697\ufe0f Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are entirely man-made. Unlike semi-synthetic opioids that are derived from compounds extracted from the opium poppy, synthetic opioids are not made from products of the opium poppy. Instead, they are made from other precursor chemicals that do not depend on the opium poppy. Synthetic opioid compounds are made to mimic natural opioids (as well as endorphins), allowing them to activate opioid receptors. Examples include **fentanyl**, **methadone**, and **tramadol**.\n\n---\n\n## How They Work\nOpioids are a class of drugs that derive from, or mimic, natural substances found in the opium poppy plant. Opioids work in the brain to produce a variety of effects, including pain relief.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, opioids ultimately trace their origins back to the **opium poppy plant**, whether they are naturally extracted, chemically modified from natural extracts, or synthetically designed to mimic the plant's compounds.",
